@* Landing — the front door, and the only screen with no content of its own.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two audiences at once: someone evaluating the work, and the journalist the
   app is built for. It serves the first without pretending not to be a
   demonstration, which is why the disclosure sits in the second paragraph
   rather than in a footer.

   NOT A MARKETING PAGE. No feature grid, no testimonials, no invented
   statistics, no logos. This is the easiest screen in the app to over-design,
   precisely because it has no real content to defer to — so it states what the
   app does, that it is simulated, and how to proceed, and then stops.

   The demo credentials are deliberately NOT here. They belong beside the form
   they are typed into, one click away. A reviewer who lands on a credential
   dump learns what to type before learning what they are looking at, and
   publishing them twice would create two places to keep in sync. *@

@page "/"
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject SimulatedSessionProvider Session

<PageTitle>FIFA Press App</PageTitle>

<h1>Know what changed about your access, before you're refused</h1>

<p class="landing__lede">
    A companion for journalists covering the 2026 World Cup. It shows what your
    accreditation currently permits, what has changed about it, and the reason
    attached to each change — so you find out from this screen rather than by
    being turned away at a barrier.
</p>

<p class="landing__disclosure" role="note">
    <strong>This is a portfolio demonstration, not a FIFA product.</strong>
    It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to FIFA. Every record,
    holder and decision in it is simulated. The match schedule is real; nothing
    else is.
</p>

<div class="landing__entries">
    <section class="landing__entry">
        <h2>Sign in with a demo account</h2>
        <p>
            Two accounts are published on the sign-in page, in plain text, with
            the passwords. They open two different holders' records — and the
            difference between those records is the thing worth seeing.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="signin">Go to sign in</a>
    </section>

    <section class="landing__entry">
        <h2>Look around without one</h2>
        <p>
            The match schedule and the help page need no account. Help is where
            the boundary of this service is stated plainly, including what it
            cannot do for you.
        </p>
        <p class="landing__public-links">
            <a href="matches">Browse the match schedule</a> ·
            <a href="help">Read what this service does not do</a>
        </p>
    </section>
</div>

<section class="landing__what-you-get">
    <h2>What a signed-in record shows</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Every change to your access, newest first</strong> — with
            what changed, why it changed, and what you can do about it. A change
            with no reason attached is not something this app will show you,
            because a change with no reason is what it exists to replace.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Warning before the consequence, where the consequence is
            foreseeable</strong> — a quarter-final allocation that depends on a
            fixture nobody has played yet is stated as a condition, naming both
            outcomes, rather than as a decision already taken.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>How old the information is, always</strong> — not only when
            it is stale. A confident headline over hours-old data is the quiet
            failure this app is built to prevent.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// A signed-in visitor has no use for the front door. Redirecting rather
    /// than rendering a second entry point keeps one answer to "where is my
    /// record", which is the whole reason the record got a named route.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        if (Session.IsSignedIn)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo("record", replace: true);
        }
    }
}
